{"question_id": "V2-dev-BOOK-en-T1-001", "approach": "LEX6", "approach_base": "LEX6", "flags": {}, "setting": "en-en", "q_lang": "en", "d_lang": "en", "corpus": "K", "corpus_set": "BOOK_en", "answer_text": "Faraday explains that the cup forms because an ascending current of air, driven by the candle's heat, cools the outer edges so they stay solid while only the interior melts.\n\n- The heat of the candle creates an upward current of air that cools all the sides of the wax or tallow, keeping the edge much cooler than the part within (evidence [2]).\n- The interior melts because the flame runs down the wick as far as it can before being extinguished, while the outside does not melt (evidence [2]).\n- The melted tallow itself extinguishes the flame at the sides, which is why the candle does not burn all down the side of the wick (evidence [1]).\n- This \"beautifully regular ascending current of air\" keeps the exterior cool and thus forms the cup; no fuel lacking this property would serve as a candle (evidence [2]).\n- If the air current is irregular, the cup is bad and the candle gutters, with fluid running over the sides (evidence [3]).", "abstained": false, "retrieved_units": [{"text": "[stripped: 774 chars]", "doc_id": "K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en", "section_id": "K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en::o029", "unit_ids": ["K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en::p1_b52", "K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en::p1_b53"], "kind": "package", "score": null, "rank": null, "span": [21708, 22482], "meta": {"chunk": 2563, "completed": 6, "prefix": 0, "provenance": [["K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en", 21708, 22482, "body"]]}}, {"text": "[stripped: 1019 chars]", "doc_id": "K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en", "section_id": "K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en::o029", "unit_ids": ["K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-h